{
  "an3.95:0.1": "Numbered Discourses 3.95 ",
  "an3.95:0.2": "10. A Lump of Salt ",
  "an3.95:0.3": "Assemblies ",
  "an3.95:1.1": "“Mendicants, these are the three assemblies. ",
  "an3.95:1.2": "What three? ",
  "an3.95:1.3": "An assembly of the best, a divided assembly, and a harmonious assembly. ",
  "an3.95:2.1": "And what is an assembly of the best? ",
  "an3.95:2.2": "An assembly where the senior mendicants are not indulgent or slack, nor are they backsliders. Instead, they take the lead in seclusion, rousing energy for attaining the unattained, achieving the unachieved, and realizing the unrealized. And those who come afterwards follow their example. ",
  "an3.95:2.3": "They too are not indulgent or slack, nor are they backsliders. Instead, they take the lead in seclusion, rousing energy for attaining the unattained, achieving the unachieved, and realizing the unrealized. ",
  "an3.95:2.4": "This is called an assembly of the best. ",
  "an3.95:3.1": "And what is a divided assembly? ",
  "an3.95:3.2": "An assembly where the mendicants fight, quarrel, and dispute, continually wounding each other with barbed words. This is called a divided assembly. ",
  "an3.95:4.1": "And what is a harmonious assembly? ",
  "an3.95:4.2": "An assembly where the mendicants live in harmony, appreciating each other, without dispute, blending like milk and water, and regarding each other with kindly eyes. This is called a harmonious assembly. ",
  "an3.95:5.1": "When the mendicants live in harmony like this, they brim with